"""Concept lessons in syllabus order. The ¶ marker separates teaching paragraphs."""
LESSONS={}
def put(n,raw):
    LESSONS[n]=[]
    for block in raw.strip().split("\n\n"):
        prose,trace=block.strip().split(" || ")
        LESSONS[n].append(dict(prose=prose.replace(" ¶ ","\n\n"),trace=trace.split(" / ")))

put(2, '''Suppose we are asked to build a social feed. Before estimating servers, we need to know what people will do: publish posts, follow accounts, read updates, or report harmful content. These journeys lead to different work inside the system. ¶ Write the required outcome for each important journey. “Keep an accepted post and show it to followers within five seconds” is specific enough to discuss and test. Then separate essential behaviour from desirable improvements. Investigate requirements whose failure would cause the greatest harm or whose feasibility is uncertain. Record exclusions too: private messaging should not silently enter an estimate for a public feed. || Journey: publish a post / Must: retain accepted post / Target: visible within 5 s / Exclude: direct messages

Opening a feed appears to be one action. In an assumed session, however, the browser sends one feed request, fetches twenty images, and records one analytics event. These requests differ in size and in the work they cause. ¶ A workload describes that mixture of operations and how often they arrive. For each operation, estimate its rate, payload size, and dependencies. A small response can still require expensive computation; an image can require little computation but substantial transfer. Keep these workloads separate when finding bottlenecks. The first estimates are assumptions. Replace them with measurements once the application is running. || 1 feed request / 20 image requests / 1 analytics event / Separate workloads and budgets

Assume users read ten feeds for every post they publish. At the public API, this looks read-heavy. But if publishing copies the new post into a thousand follower feeds, that one action causes a thousand additional storage writes. ¶ A read/write ratio therefore needs a boundary: are we counting user actions, API requests, or database operations? Creating follower feeds in advance moves work to publication time. Building them when requested moves work to reading time. The choice depends on how often users publish and read. Read replicas can help some reads, but delayed replicas may not immediately show a user's new post. || 1 publish action / 1 durable post write / 1000 follower-feed writes / Logical ratio differs from storage ratio

Suppose a server receives 200 requests each second and completes 180. If the remaining requests stay queued, twenty more accumulate every second. Reporting only the completion rate would hide the growing wait. ¶ Latency is the time one operation takes. Throughput is the amount of work completed per unit time. RPS usually means requests per second, QPS queries per second, and TPS transactions per second. State which work each number counts, since one request may cause several queries. Measure offered work, successful completions, errors, and rejected requests together. Otherwise, an apparently steady throughput can conceal users who are waiting or giving up. || Offered: 200 requests/s / Completed: 180 requests/s / Backlog: +20 requests/s / Measure rejection and timeout too

Consider 100 assumed request times: 99 requests take 10 milliseconds each, and one takes 1,000 milliseconds. The mean is (99×10 + 1,000)/100 = 19.9 milliseconds. That average tells us little about the user who waited a full second. ¶ The median describes the middle of a sorted set. A percentile such as p95 or p99 describes a point near its upper end; the precise estimate depends on the percentile convention. Use the distribution to study slow requests. To find a fleet-wide p99, combine compatible observations or histograms. Averaging individual servers' p99 values does not produce the fleet p99. Account for timeouts rather than discarding them. || 99 requests at 10 ms / 1 request at 1000 ms / Mean = 19.9 ms / Tail experience remains 1000 ms

A payment status page can respond quickly while showing yesterday's information. Its ability to respond and the freshness of its answer are separate properties. ¶ Availability concerns providing the required service when needed. Durability concerns whether committed data survives specified failures. Reliability usually concerns correct service over time; state how it is being measured. Consistency defines which observations of state are allowed. For example, must the next read show a completed payment? Choose the requirement for each operation. A popularity count may tolerate delayed updates, while selling the last ticket requires stronger coordination. A single label such as “reliable database” leaves these decisions unanswered. || Request responds successfully / Response may be stale / Acknowledged state may be lost / Test each promise separately

Suppose a service handles one million eligible requests in a month. A target of 99.9% successful requests allows 0.1%, or 1,000, to fail. This allowance is its event-based error budget for that population and period. ¶ An SLI is the indicator we measure, such as the fraction of successful requests. An SLO is its target and measurement window. An SLA is an agreement with stated obligations and possibly consequences. Define eligible requests honestly; excluding failures merely to improve the number defeats the purpose. A time-based objective uses time as its denominator and gives a different budget. Use budget consumption to guide release and reliability work, while also considering the harm of a concentrated outage. || 1000000 eligible events / Target: 99.9% good / Allowance: 1000 bad events / Track rate of budget consumption

Suppose stored data grows by an assumed 10% each month. Starting with one unit, a year of this growth gives 1.1¹² ≈ 3.14 units. That is a planning scenario, not evidence that request traffic and CPU usage will also triple. ¶ Plan each important resource separately: stored history, frequently accessed data, background jobs, and spare capacity during failures. Include temporary space needed while old and new data layouts coexist during a migration. A useful capacity plan names the resource likely to fill first, the time needed to add capacity, and the observation that triggers action. Revisit the assumptions as measurements arrive. || Starting volume: 1 unit / Monthly multiplier: 1.1 / After 12 months: 3.14 units / Recheck traffic and resource mix

Assume ten million daily users each make fifty API requests. The daily total is 10,000,000×50 = 500,000,000 requests. Divide by 86,400 seconds per day to obtain about 5,787 requests per second on average. ¶ If each user publishes one 2 kB post daily, raw post growth is 20 GB/day. If each views ten 200 kB images, image delivery totals 20 TB/day. These calculations use decimal units and describe different resources. Add indexes, extra copies, retained history, and transfer overhead separately. Memory depends on the data kept active; CPU depends on the work per request. Neither can be inferred from the user count alone. These are assumed workloads, not company benchmarks. || API: 500 million requests/day / Posts: 20 GB raw/day / Images: 20 TB delivered/day / Add indexes, replicas and headroom

A daily average spreads all requests evenly across 24 hours. Real users may arrive together after a notification or event. Suppose, for planning, that peak API traffic is five times the preceding average. The estimate becomes 5×5,787.04 ≈ 28,935 requests per second. ¶ Also ask how long the peak lasts. Extra instances that take several minutes to start may arrive after a short spike has ended. Admission limits and existing spare capacity must handle the gap. Image traffic and posting traffic may peak differently, so a common multiplier is only an initial assumption. Retries during an outage can create another peak that ordinary user averages miss. || Average: 5787 requests/s / Assumed peak multiplier: 5 / Peak: 28935 requests/s / Scaling delay still needs protection

Imagine a stable service completing 500 operations per second. Each operation spends an average of 40 milliseconds inside the boundary being measured. Little's Law gives average work in progress as L = λW: 500/s × 0.04 s = 20 operations. ¶ Here L is the average number in the system, λ the long-run arrival/completion rate, and W the average time inside it. If that average time rises to 0.4 seconds while stable throughput stays 500/s, L becomes 200. Use consistent boundaries and units, including waiting time where applicable. This relation describes stable averages; it does not predict p99 or make an overloaded queue become stable. || Arrival/completion rate: 500/s / Mean residence: 0.04 s / Mean work in flight: 20 / At 0.4 s: 200 in flight

Suppose a job spends 20% of its time in work that must run sequentially. The other 80% can be divided perfectly among workers. With four workers, the ideal time becomes 0.2 + 0.8/4 = 0.4 of the original. The speedup is therefore 1/0.4 = 2.5 times. ¶ This is Amdahl's Law for a fixed amount of work. Even infinitely many workers cannot remove the sequential 20%, so the ideal ceiling is five times. Coordination can reduce the real benefit further. Find the stage that limits progress before adding workers. After improving it, measure again: another stage may become the new bottleneck. || Serial fraction: 0.2 / Parallel fraction: 0.8 / 4 workers: 2.5x ideal speedup / Infinite workers: 5x ceiling

A page request may start fifty calls in parallel and need all their results. Its completion then depends on the slowest required call. Assume each call independently has a 1% chance of exceeding a threshold. The chance that all are fast is 0.99⁵⁰; the chance that at least one is slow is 1−0.99⁵⁰ ≈ 39.5%. ¶ This example shows how fan-out can worsen tail latency. Shared dependencies can also correlate delays, so independence must be justified. Divide the overall deadline among network travel, waiting, computation, and returning the reply. Sending duplicate calls to reduce waiting spends extra capacity and is appropriate only when repeated execution is safe. || 50 required parallel calls / Per-call slow probability: 0.01 / All fast probability: 0.99^50 / At least one slow: about 39.5%

Suppose two designs both handle the expected traffic. One is cheaper but allows a tenant's cached result to reach another tenant. It fails a security requirement and should be rejected before comparing prices. ¶ Other differences may be preferences: one design costs more money but takes less operating work. Include staffing, maintenance, migration, and recovery in the comparison. A team also needs the skills to run what it builds. Compare candidates against the same workload and failure requirements. A price advantage obtained by dropping backups or weakening isolation represents a different service, rather than a like-for-like saving. || Check mandatory correctness / Check security and operation limits / Estimate total lifecycle cost / Compare feasible candidates

After rejecting designs that fail mandatory requirements, we may still have several reasonable choices. A decision matrix lists the criteria, gives each candidate a score, and weights the criteria according to their importance. It makes the team's judgments visible. ¶ Try changing uncertain scores and weights. If a small change reverses the result, the decision needs better evidence or a choice that is easy to reverse. Record the reason and the condition for reconsidering it in an ADR. The weighted total helps discussion; tests are still needed to establish performance and correctness. A high score cannot make an unsupported requirement true. || Reject infeasible candidates / Score explicit preferences / Vary uncertain weights / Record choice and revisit trigger''')

put(3, '''Type a website address into a browser. Before the page appears, the browser may resolve the name, establish a connection, negotiate security, send a request, and fetch additional resources. The server must also perform its application work, and the browser must render the result. ¶ Some steps can reuse earlier work. A cached address avoids a lookup; an existing connection avoids setup. Follow the path used by the actual request before assigning a single “network time.” A fast server can still produce a slow page when images are large or connection setup takes several round trips. The request is a sequence of dependent activities. || URL parsed / Name and route resolved / Secure connection used / Response parsed and rendered

To contact a web server, a client needs an address and a transport port. Routing moves packets toward the destination address. The port helps the destination operating system direct transport traffic to the appropriate endpoint. A socket is an endpoint the operating system provides for communication. ¶ An application might listen on port 443 but remain unreachable because it is bound to the wrong address, a firewall blocks the path, or replies cannot return. Investigate the whole route. An address or port also provides no proof of permission: reaching a service and being authorised to use it are different requirements. Private addressing alone does not establish that a caller is trusted. || Destination name to address / Route packets toward host / Transport selects port 443 / Application accepts a connection

Suppose an application sends two messages. With TCP, the receiver gets an ordered stream of bytes; it must use an application rule to discover where each message ends. UDP provides datagrams but does not supply TCP's ordered, reliable stream behaviour. ¶ QUIC runs over UDP and provides its own secure transport with separate streams and connection identifiers. Loss affecting one stream need not block delivery on another as a missing TCP byte can. The streams still share congestion constraints and may have application dependencies. Choose a transport by the required behaviour and supported infrastructure. Using UDP does not by itself make an application faster, reliable, or secure. || Application writes messages / TCP exposes ordered bytes / UDP exposes datagrams / QUIC exposes secure streams

A browser knows a name such as example.org, but it needs a usable network address. A DNS resolver obtains the relevant records, often using cached answers rather than asking authoritative servers each time. A record's time to live, or TTL, guides how long it may be cached. ¶ Now change the address during failover. Some clients still hold the earlier answer; others already have open connections and do no new lookup. Negative answers may also be cached. The change therefore does not redirect every client immediately. Test DNS refresh and connection lifetimes together when designing failover. The authoritative record is only one part of how clients choose a destination. || Name lookup / Cached answer valid until expiry / Record changes at authority / Existing clients may retain old route

When a browser opens an HTTPS connection, it needs to establish that it is talking to the intended server and protect the traffic. TLS provides peer authentication under its trust configuration, along with confidentiality and integrity protection. The client must validate the certificate and its relationship to the requested hostname. ¶ Trace where encryption ends. If a reverse proxy terminates TLS, the proxy-to-application connection needs its own policy. Protect the relevant keys as well. TLS protects the connection; the application must still check whether a signed-in user may read a particular task. A secure transport does not supply resource ownership rules. || Client validates server identity / Peers establish traffic keys / Encrypted application records / Application still checks authorization

A page often needs many resources. HTTP/1.1 clients may use multiple connections to avoid waiting behind other responses. HTTP/2 carries multiple streams over one TCP connection. If a TCP byte is lost, later bytes wait for recovery, which can delay several HTTP/2 streams. ¶ HTTP/3 uses QUIC streams and changes that transport behaviour. The protocols retain HTTP's application meanings, such as methods and status codes, while carrying messages differently. Performance depends on loss, negotiation, proxies, and payloads. Upgrading the protocol can reduce some waiting, but the request still spends time in application queues and the database. Measure the complete path rather than attributing every delay to the HTTP version. || Same resource semantics / HTTP/1.1 connection scheduling / HTTP/2 streams over TCP / HTTP/3 streams over QUIC

The first request to a server may need a new connection; a later request can reuse one. A new TCP connection normally performs a handshake, and secure setup adds exchanges whose number depends on the protocol and reuse options. Existing connections avoid some of this work. ¶ Connection reuse needs limits. A connection left idle may be closed by an intermediary. A very long-lived connection may continue using an endpoint that discovery has replaced. Conversely, closing connections too often wastes setup work and CPU. Choose lifetimes and pool sizes that fit the infrastructure and workload, and handle replacement safely when an old connection no longer works. || Cold request needs setup / Warm request reuses connection / Idle intermediaries may expire it / Pool validates or replaces connection

Assume a simplified cold request spends 20 ms on DNS, 40 ms on TCP setup, 40 ms on TLS setup, and 55 ms on the request, server work, and reply. In this sequential model, the total is 20+40+40+55 = 155 ms. ¶ This is an assumed example, not a benchmark. Cached DNS, connection reuse, security resumption, and overlapping work change the calculation. It illustrates why a small amount of server computation can still accompany substantial delay. Moving a service closer can reduce network travel time. Reuse or batching can reduce the number of exchanges. First identify which exchanges the actual request needs. || DNS: 20 ms / TCP setup: 40 ms / TLS setup: 40 ms / Request plus work: 55 ms

An organisation may send employees' outbound traffic through a forward proxy. A website may place a reverse proxy in front of its application servers. The first acts on the client side of the exchange; the second presents the servers to incoming clients. ¶ A reverse proxy can terminate TLS, route requests, and apply limits, but it also introduces another component that can fail or time out. Decide which forwarded metadata the application may trust. For example, accepting a client-supplied identity header as if a trusted proxy had checked it can bypass authentication. The design must identify who creates the header and which callers can reach the receiving endpoint. || Client may choose forward proxy / Reverse proxy fronts application / Proxy forwards selected metadata / Application validates trusted boundary

Suppose incoming requests must be sent to one of several application instances. A load balancer chooses a backend. An API gateway often adds API-specific functions such as authentication integration, quotas, or request transformation. A product can perform both roles. ¶ Assign responsibilities explicitly instead of relying on product names. A gateway can reject an oversized request or apply a coarse rate limit, while a domain service checks ownership of a payment. Moving every business rule into the gateway makes many teams depend on changes there. Also trace downstream capacity: distributing traffic across more application instances does not repair a database that is already overloaded. || Edge receives request / Gateway applies boundary policy / Balancer chooses healthy backend / Domain service enforces resource rules

To route a connection by its destination port, a balancer can work with transport information: this is Layer-4 routing. To route an HTTP request by its host or path, it must inspect application messages: this is Layer-7 routing. ¶ Application-aware routing supports richer decisions but adds parsing, processing, and trust requirements. The placement of encryption termination determines which component can see those messages. Also distinguish requests from connections. A long-lived connection can remain attached to one backend even while new connections go elsewhere. Choose the routing level by the information needed for the decision and the connection behaviour the application expects. || L4: address and transport port / L7: host, path, headers / Select backend under policy / Existing stream retains affinity

Assume 1,000 clients ask for updates every five seconds. Even when nothing changes, polling creates 1,000/5 = 200 requests per second. Each response gives the client another snapshot. ¶ Server-Sent Events lets a server stream events to a client over HTTP. WebSockets supports messaging in both directions after connection setup. Streaming avoids repeated empty polls but keeps connections open. Plan heartbeats, buffers, reconnects, and what happens to missed updates. A live connection is a delivery path, not a durable message history. If clients must recover updates after a disconnection, the application needs event identities and a replay or resynchronisation rule. || 1000 clients / Poll every 5 seconds / Baseline: 200 requests/s / Streaming trades polls for connection state

A client opens an outbound connection, but no reply arrives. The problem may lie outside the remote application: a firewall may block traffic, or a network address translation table may have exhausted or expired its mappings. ¶ NAT rewrites network addressing. A firewall applies traffic rules. Ingress is traffic entering a boundary, and egress is traffic leaving it. Show these functions when tracing the path in and out. Choose connection lifetimes and outbound policies together. Segmentation can reduce which services are reachable, but an allowed connection must still authenticate and authorise its requests, particularly in a shared tenant environment. || Outbound socket allocated / NAT mapping and firewall policy / Remote response returns / Expired state can break the path

An application wants to call “the payment service” even as instances are replaced. Service discovery maps that logical name to usable endpoints. It may rely on DNS, a registry, or the platform's control plane. ¶ Discovery information can be stale. An instance might fail after being advertised, or a healthy instance might already lack spare capacity. Cache results to avoid making every call depend on a lookup, but refresh them deliberately. A client must still cope with a bad endpoint, using bounded retries within its deadline. Successful discovery establishes a possible destination; it does not establish that the operation will succeed. || Instance registers / Discovery publishes endpoints / Client selects cached endpoint / Failure requires bounded refresh and retry

A user requests a public image. A nearby CDN cache can return it without contacting the origin server. On a miss, the edge fetches the image and stores a representation according to its caching policy. ¶ The cache key determines which requests share a copy, and freshness rules determine when that copy may be reused. These are correctness decisions. A missing tenant or authorisation dimension can make a cache serve someone else's content. Public images and private account pages need different policies. CDNs reduce some network travel and origin traffic, while adding copies whose expiry, purge, and failure behaviour must be understood. || Browser requests asset / Nearby edge checks cache key / Miss fetches origin / Cached representation has freshness policy

A client sends a request and waits until its deadline. It sees a timeout. Did the server receive the request, perform it, or commit its write? The timeout alone cannot answer these questions. The request or its reply may have been delayed or lost. ¶ A network partition prevents some participants from communicating even while other paths work. Treat such partial failures as normal possibilities. Distinguish connection-setup, idle, and overall request limits. Unbounded waiting can exhaust resources; blind retrying can repeat an effect. Use operation identifiers or status queries where the client needs to recover an uncertain outcome, and keep retries inside an overall deadline. || Request may reach server / Server may commit / Reply may be lost / Caller sees only a timeout

A database connection pool lets requests borrow existing connections. Suppose there are twenty connections and each is held for an assumed average of 50 ms. If every connection remains busy, the simple service-capacity calculation is 20/0.05 = 400 operations per second before overhead and variability. ¶ A request can also wait to borrow a connection, so include that wait in its deadline. Oversizing the pool may merely overload the database; a leaked connection gradually reduces usable capacity. Keepalive can help detect some broken connections, but says little about whether a business operation will succeed. Measure queueing and hold times before changing the pool size. || Pool capacity: 20 / Mean hold time: 0.05 s / Ideal ceiling: 400 operations/s / Queue and timeout before exhaustion''')

put(4, '''The same program can run twice at the same time. Each running instance is a process, with its own address space and resources. Inside a process, threads provide separate paths of execution. They commonly share heap memory but have their own stacks and scheduling state. ¶ These runtime boundaries differ from source files and modules. A crash of one process can stop all its threads, even if they execute different modules. Separate processes can provide useful isolation, while threads make sharing data convenient. Sharing introduces coordination needs: neither choosing threads nor dividing code into classes automatically makes concurrent updates safe. || Program on disk / Process owns address space / Threads share process resources / Process failure removes its threads

When an upload request calls functions, the runtime keeps call information on a stack. Dynamically managed objects, such as a large upload buffer, commonly live on the heap. The details depend on the language and runtime. ¶ An object's lifetime need not end when the initiating function returns. If a background task still holds a reference to the buffer, the buffer can remain in memory. Track who keeps each resource and when that ownership ends. Files and database connections need timely cleanup as well. A garbage collector managing memory does not establish when every external resource is closed. Bound the number and size of outstanding uploads. || Request allocates buffer / Task retains reference / Request returns / Buffer remains until ownership ends

A machine may have more runnable threads than available cores. Its scheduler chooses which thread runs next. A context switch saves one execution state and restores another, and may disturb useful CPU-cache contents. ¶ More threads help when some are waiting and others can use an idle CPU. When the CPU is already saturated, more runnable threads can add overhead and waiting. A blocked I/O call can leave the CPU free for another thread; a busy-wait loop consumes it. Inspect CPU utilisation, runnable queues, and lock contention before choosing a thread count. Starting a thread for every incoming request allows resource use to grow without a bound. || Runnable work queues / CPU runs one context / Context switch restores another / Too many runnable tasks add overhead

Assume 1,000 uploads each spend 100 ms waiting for storage and require negligible CPU work. Handling them sequentially takes about 100 seconds of waiting. Allowing fifty independent waits at once gives an ideal waiting-time lower bound near two seconds, before overhead and bandwidth limits. ¶ This workload is I/O-bound: waiting is the main limit. Compression may instead be CPU-bound because useful time is spent computing. Extra asynchronous tasks do not create extra CPU cores. For computation, investigate algorithms and actual parallel execution. For waiting, concurrency can use idle periods, provided the storage service and connections can support it. Measure which resource limits the real workload. || 1000 uploads / 100 ms assumed wait each / Sequential wait sum: 100 s / 50 concurrent waits: about 2 s ideal

A caller can start an operation and wait for its result before proceeding; this is synchronous interaction. With an asynchronous interface, initiation and later completion are represented separately, so the caller can arrange other work while waiting. ¶ A different question is whether the thread itself blocks. An async function can still call a blocking library operation and stall the thread running it. Trace the implementation beneath the interface, including drivers. Choose a model that keeps waiting and resource usage manageable for the workload. The word “async” describes an interface or execution arrangement; it does not establish that every operation underneath is nonblocking. || Start operation / Caller may await completion / Underlying call may still block / Inspect runtime and driver behavior

An event loop starts a network read and arranges to be notified when progress is possible. While the I/O waits, the loop can run another ready task. Nonblocking I/O avoids holding the thread idle throughout that wait. ¶ The mechanism still needs readiness or completion notifications. Repeatedly checking an unfinished operation without yielding can consume CPU instead of saving it. Use the platform's intended interface. If a required library blocks, it may need a separate, bounded executor. Limit both active blocking jobs and queued jobs, and define cancellation and cleanup. Nonblocking submission does not mean the requested work has finished. || Submit I/O / Register readiness or completion / Run another ready task / Resume when operation can progress

Imagine two uploads on one core. Upload A starts, then waits for storage. The core works on B during that wait. Both activities are in progress over overlapping periods, so they are concurrent. ¶ If two cores execute A and B at the same instant, there is parallelism as well. Concurrency concerns overlapping progress; parallelism concerns simultaneous execution. A one-core program can still have a race if operations are interleaved between a read and its matching write. Decide separately how much work may be in progress and how much can execute at once. Both choices affect resource use, while coordination determines correctness. || Task A starts / Task A waits while B runs / Activities overlap in time / Multiple cores can execute simultaneously

A task awaits a future representing a pending result. The event loop runs other ready work. When the result becomes available, the task's continuation becomes eligible to run. A promise or future is the runtime's representation of that later result; exact behaviour depends on the runtime. ¶ Now suppose one callback computes continuously for two seconds on the only event-loop thread. Other ready callbacks must wait, even if their I/O already finished. Keep long computation away from the loop or split work appropriately. Scheduling a future also does not make the work durable: if its process dies, a memory-only task may disappear. || Task awaits future / Loop runs ready work / Completion makes continuation ready / Long callback delays other tasks

Suppose eight workers process uploads. The pool limits active execution to eight jobs, but accepting a thousand large buffers into a waiting queue can still exhaust memory. Execution limits and admission limits solve different problems. ¶ Use a bounded queue as well as a bounded worker count. Where practical, store large upload bytes separately and queue compact references. Decide what happens when the queue is full: reject, wait until a deadline, or use another supported path. A durable queue is needed if accepting the work promises survival across process loss. Count queued payloads, active buffers, and open resources when choosing the limits. || Bounded input queue / 8 workers execute jobs / Queue stores object references / Full queue triggers admission policy

Two threads can update a shared inventory map directly, provided they coordinate access. Alternatively, one owner can keep the map private and process inventory commands sent by other tasks. These are shared-memory and message-passing approaches. ¶ A single owner can make some local rules easier to preserve, but its messages may repeat and the owner can fail. Define delivery and recovery behaviour. Sending a pointer to an object that both sides can still mutate has not transferred exclusive ownership. Choose the arrangement by where state belongs and how much communication it needs. Both approaches need clear rules for concurrent access and failure. || Shared counter needs coordination / Owner receives commands / Owner updates private state / Messages can still repeat or fail

Start a counter at zero. A reads zero. B reads zero before A writes. A adds one and writes one; B also adds one to its earlier value and writes one. Two increments have produced a final value of one. ¶ This is a lost update caused by a race: the outcome depends on how operations interleave. Protect the complete read–modify–write sequence as a critical section, using a suitable lock or atomic operation. Locking only the final assignment leaves the earlier reads unprotected. The protection must be shared by all relevant writers. A mutex inside one process cannot coordinate unrelated processes writing a database. || A reads 0 / B reads 0 / A writes 1 / B writes 1: increment lost

Different coordination tools answer different questions. A mutex allows one holder into a protected section. A semaphore permits a limited number of holders. An atomic operation makes a specified action indivisible under its defined memory-order rules. ¶ A condition variable lets a thread wait for a condition while releasing a lock, then reacquire it before proceeding. Recheck the condition in a loop: waking does not establish that it is still true. Choose the protected state by the invariant. If a transfer changes two balances together, making each balance individually atomic does not automatically make the whole transfer atomic. || Acquire lock / Check predicate in loop / Wait releases and reacquires lock / Change state and notify appropriately

A holds the inventory lock and waits for the payment lock. B holds the payment lock and waits for inventory. Neither can continue. This circular wait is a deadlock. ¶ Starvation occurs when a participant repeatedly fails to obtain the service or resource it needs. Livelock occurs when participants remain active but make no useful progress, for example by repeatedly yielding and retrying in the same pattern. Consistent lock ordering can prevent the illustrated deadlock. Shorter critical sections or different ownership may help too. A timeout needs cleanup and rollback; repeating the same failed coordination indefinitely can merely replace one problem with another. || A holds inventory lock / B holds payment lock / A waits for payment / B waits for inventory: cycle

A thread constructs an object and publishes a reference to another thread. For the reader to use it correctly, the language's memory model must guarantee visibility of the initialised fields. An earlier wall-clock time alone does not supply that guarantee. ¶ Happens-before is an ordering relation used by memory models to describe which effects must be visible. Synchronisation mechanisms, such as an appropriate channel or lock, can establish the required relationship. Use the rules of the actual language and runtime rather than assuming all machines behave the same way. The Go memory model is one specified example. Safe publication matters even when the object will not change afterward. || Writer initializes object / Synchronizing publish operation / Reader synchronizes receive / Reader observes specified initialized state

Suppose task records never change after construction. Readers can share those immutable values without competing to modify their fields. But a mutable dictionary that holds the records can still have concurrent inserts and removals. ¶ Thread safety is a claim about allowed concurrent use of the whole operation or object. Immutability removes some mutation problems; publication and surrounding containers still need coordination. Other approaches include keeping data inside one owner, copying values, or using locks. State which access is allowed and who protects it. Returning a writable internal collection lets callers bypass the rules even if each record looks safe on its own. || Construct immutable value / Publish through defined boundary / Readers do not mutate value / Mutable container still needs coordination

Assume jobs arrive at 150 per second while workers complete 100 per second. If every job is accepted, the waiting queue grows by fifty each second. A larger queue postpones running out of space but leaves the mismatch unchanged. ¶ Backpressure slows or limits upstream work according to downstream capacity. Use bounded queues, admission deadlines, and clear overload responses. Keep queued payloads compact. Monitor the age of the oldest job as well as the number waiting: a short queue of stuck jobs may still be harmful. When a dependency slows, unlimited concurrency turns its delay into memory, connection, and thread exhaustion elsewhere. || Arrivals: 150 jobs/s / Completion: 100 jobs/s / Backlog grows: 50 jobs/s / Bound queue and reject excess work

An upload request starts several child tasks. If its deadline expires, someone must cancel work that is no longer needed and release local resources. Structured concurrency gives those child tasks a parent scope responsible for their completion and failure. ¶ Cancellation is a request to stop, with behaviour defined by the runtime and operation. It cannot prove that a remote side effect was undone. A payment may already have committed. Use reliable local cleanup for files and connections, and a status or reconciliation process for uncertain remote effects. If accepted work must continue after the request process dies, give it a durable job record and an explicit owner. || Parent request starts children / Deadline cancels owned tasks / Cleanup releases local resources / Reconcile external effects separately''')
